<body>
<h1>Unit 4: Dynamics</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Dynamics supplies the causes. Newton's three laws are the whole of classical mechanics for point masses, but the ways they are applied - free-body diagrams, variable mass, self-adjusting friction - decide whether an answer survives contact with reality.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>The first law defines an inertial frame: one in which a free body keeps constant velocity, and the second law then reads F = ma.</li><li>F = ma is the constant-mass form. For a changing mass the correct statement is F = dp/dt, which is what lets a rocket accelerate.</li><li>Third-law pairs act on different bodies, are equal and opposite, and share the same line of interaction.</li><li>Mass measures inertia and is independent of g; weight is the gravitational force mg, so weight changes with location while mass does not.</li><li>A free-body diagram isolates one body and contains only interactions, never 'forces of motion'.</li><li>Static friction is self-adjusting, 0 &lt;= f &lt;= mu N; only at impending slip does it reach mu N, which is why pulling a box at constant speed needs less force than starting it.</li><li>Newton's second law is a rate law: the force at an instant fixes the change of momentum, so a larger force acting for a shorter time can deliver exactly the same impulse.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>F = ma assumes constant mass in an inertial frame; rockets, raindrops gaining mass from vapour, and chains picking up links all violate it.</li><li>Textbook problems neglect air resistance and buoyancy; both become dominant for light bodies, fine dust and falling feathers.</li><li>The classic 'lift and the Earth' third-law discussion fails because the force on the person and the force on the ground are not a third-law pair: the true partner of the person-Newton force is the person-Earth force.</li><li>Friction coefficients are empirical, depend on surface condition, cleanliness, humidity and sliding speed, and are not universal constants of the two materials.</li><li>Newton's laws assume an inertial frame and classical, instantaneous action; they hold to about one part in 10^10 of the speed of light and stop being fundamental at relativistic and quantum scales.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 5 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Linear momentum, impulse</li><li>Conservation of linear momentum</li><li>Application of Newton's laws</li><li>Moment, torque and equilibrium</li><li>Solid friction: laws of solid friction and their verifications</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The reaction force a table exerts on a book is equal and opposite to the weight of the book, so the net force on the book is zero.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The action and reaction in a third-law pair act on different bodies. The table pushes up on the book and the book pushes down on the table; they do not cancel on the book. The forces that cancel on the book are its weight and the normal reaction.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Third-law pairs never cancel each other because they act on different objects. On the book the forces are weight (down) and normal reaction (up); these are not a third-law pair, and they balance only when the book has no vertical acceleration.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A body moving at constant velocity must have no forces acting on it.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> That is the first law, and it holds only in an inertial frame. A body going round a curve at constant speed has a real net force, and a body sliding on a rough surface with constant velocity has a net force of zero only if friction exactly balances the applied force.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> In an inertial frame a body has constant velocity if and only if the resultant force on it is zero. In a non-inertial (rotating or accelerating) frame a body at rest still needs a real or fictitious force to keep it at rest.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Friction always opposes motion.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Static friction opposes the tendency to slip, not motion. On a book sliding on a table friction opposes the motion; on a book on a accelerating trolley that slides forward without tipping, friction accelerates the book forward.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Friction opposes relative motion at the contact. Static friction is self-adjusting with 0 &lt;= f &lt;= mu_s N and only reaches mu_s N at impending slip; kinetic friction is approximately mu_k N and opposes the sliding.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A 5.0 kg block rests on a rough horizontal surface with coefficient of friction 0.40. A horizontal force of 50 N is applied. Find the acceleration of the block.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Normal reaction N = m g = 5.0 x 9.8 = 49 N. Limiting friction = mu N = 0.40 x 49 = 19.6 N, which is less than the applied 50 N, so the block slides. Net force = 50 - 19.6 = 30.4 N, so a = 30.4 / 5.0 = 6.08 m/s^2 (about 6.1 m/s^2).</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Show that the impulse of a constant force acting for a time t equals the change in momentum of the body it acts on.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Newton's second law in momentum form is F = dp/dt. For a constant force, integrating over the interval from t1 to t2 gives the impulse J = integral F dt = F (t2 - t1) = p(t2) - p(t1) = change in momentum. The same result follows for a variable force, where the impulse is the area under the force-time graph; it is then the average force times t that equals the change in momentum.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A bus brakes sharply and a standing passenger falls forward. Explain this using Newton's laws, and say which law the passenger's reaction illustrates.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> When the bus decelerates, the floor exerts a backward friction force on the passenger's feet. The passenger's body would continue at the original velocity (first law) except that the feet are held back, so the body moves forward relative to the bus. The floor's forward force on the feet and the backward force of the body on the floor are a third-law pair, and the forward motion of the body is the reaction to the backward friction on the feet. A passenger who is seated and belted does not fall because the belt supplies the force needed to decelerate with the bus.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Newton's gravitational constant (exact)</td><td>6.67430e-11 m^3 kg^-1 s^-2</td></tr><tr><td>Earth's mass</td><td>5.972e24 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Force needed to accelerate 1 kg at 1 g</td><td>about 9.81 N</td></tr><tr><td>Impulse of a car decelerating from 50 m/s in 0.05 s</td><td>about 250 N s per kg of mass</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Do smooth, globally defined solutions of the three-dimensional Navier-Stokes equations exist and remain smooth?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>The Clay Mathematics Institute announced an apparent solution in September 2026 and states that the work is still to be analysed and evaluated; it should be presented as announced, not as settled.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can the transition from laminar to turbulent flow be predicted from first principles?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>Direct numerical simulation still reaches only moderate Reynolds numbers, so the transition statistics remain an active research target.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Draw free-body diagrams for a lift accelerating upward with total mass m and explain both readings of the word 'weight'.</li><li>Show that the third law does not prevent a rocket from accelerating: track the momentum carried away by the exhaust.</li><li>Estimate the terminal velocity of a raindrop using a quadratic drag law and compare the estimate with observation.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/dynamics">Read the chapter notes: Dynamics</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-laws">Laws of motion theory lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-dynamics">Dynamics 3D lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/4-introduction-to-dynamics-newtons-laws-of-motion">OpenStax: Newton's laws</a></li><li><a href="https://www.claymath.org/millennium/navier-stokes-equation/">Clay: the Navier-Stokes problem</a></li><li><a href="https://www.claymath.org/news/navier-stokes-announcement/">Clay: Navier-Stokes announcement, 11 September 2026</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-3-newtons-second-law">OpenStax: Newton's Second Law</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
